2. EPHEMERIS DATA (FEPK coefficient packs)
==============================================================================

The packs (data/packs/*.bin, not stored in the repository; built with
tools/build_pack.py, tools/build_ext_pack.py, tools/fit_minor.py) contain
Chebyshev coefficients copied or re-fitted from the following JPL products.
JPL ephemeris data are published by NASA/JPL without licence restrictions
(see https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/naif/rules.html and https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/).
Attribution wording to be confirmed with JPL SSD before public redistribution
of built packs (MINOR_BODIES_REPORT.md, open item 5).

2.1 JPL planetary and lunar ephemerides DE441 and DE442
    NASA/Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology.
    Reference: R. S. Park, W. M. Folkner, J. G. Williams & D. H. Boggs (2021),
    "The JPL Planetary and Lunar Ephemerides DE440 and DE441", AJ 161, 105.
    DE442: JPL, https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/ftp/eph/planets/bsp/ (see the kernel
    comments, de442_tech-comments.txt).
    Suggested credit: "Planetary and lunar positions from the JPL DE441 and
    DE442 ephemerides (NASA/JPL-Caltech)."

2.2 JPL small-body ephemerides
    - sb441-n16 (Ceres, Pallas, Juno, Vesta; the 16 asteroid perturbers
      integrated with DE441), JPL IOM 392R-21-005,
      https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/ftp/eph/small_bodies/asteroids_de441/
    - JPL Horizons small-body SPK files for Chiron (2060), Pholus (5145),
      Eris (136199) and Eros (433); solutions and checksums are recorded in
      docs/MINOR_PROVENANCE.json.
    Credit: "JPL Horizons On-Line Ephemeris System, Solar System Dynamics
    Group, JPL/Caltech" and Park et al. (2021), AJ 161, 105.
    The extension of Chiron/Pholus/Eris/Eros before 1600 is FrenchEphemeris's
    own numerical integration started from the JPL states.


